    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" (which enables the interface) is to *not* set the \"disable\" flag on the interface. However, since the request was to translate \"no shutdown\" (which enables the interface), the Juniper equivalent would be to remove/not have the \"disable\" statement, but since we must provide a positive configuration command, the closest inverse is shown above. \\n\\nA more accurate representation would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable\\n##\\nas this actively removes any shutdown/disable state.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" (which enables the interface) is to *not* set the \"disable\" flag on the interface. However, since the request was to translate \"no shutdown\" (which enables the interface), the Juniper equivalent would be to remove/not have the \"disable\" statement, but since we must provide a positive configuration command, the closest inverse is shown above. \\n\\nA more accurate representation would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable\\n##\\nas this actively removes any shutdown/disable state.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['ethernet cfm domain [parameter1] level [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md3 level 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md3 level 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco CFM domain configuration command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md3 level 3\\n##\\n\\nThis Juniper command creates a CFM domain named \"md3\" with level 3, which matches the Cisco configuration. The main differences are:\\n1. The command path starts with \"protocols\" in Juniper\\n2. The CFM functionality is under \"oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management\" hierarchy\\n3. The syntax uses spaces instead of dashes/hyphens for parameter separation', 'trans_template': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md3 level 3'}]]": 1,
    "['ethernet cfm service [parameter1] vlan [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 level 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 level 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent for the Cisco CFM service configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 level 3\\nset protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 maintenance-association ma3 vlan-id-list 2\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper configuration requires two commands to achieve the same functionality:\\n1. First creates the maintenance domain with level 3 (equivalent to Cisco's md3 level 3)\\n2. Then creates the maintenance association (service) within that domain and associates it with VLAN 2\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 level 3'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 maintenance-association ma3 vlan-id-list 2', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 maintenance-association ma3 vlan-id-list 2', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent for the Cisco CFM service configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 level 3\\nset protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 maintenance-association ma3 vlan-id-list 2\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper configuration requires two commands to achieve the same functionality:\\n1. First creates the maintenance domain with level 3 (equivalent to Cisco's md3 level 3)\\n2. Then creates the maintenance association (service) within that domain and associates it with VLAN 2\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md3 maintenance-association ma3 vlan-id-list 2'}]]": 1,
